; `try` reads the error type out of the instance table, and a generic body has
; no entry in it — the `Result` here is still an application, not an instance
; (`D-095`). It refuses with a diagnostic rather than miscompiling, and
; `and-then` is the answer in the meantime.

(take std:prelude Result)

(fn chain (T E) ((value (Result T E))) -> (Result T E)
  (let inner (try value))
  (Result:Ok inner))

(fn main () -> i32 0)
